y {total_cap}\")\n    # Initialize allocations\n    allocations = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n    # While there are tokens to allocate\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_index = None\n        best_score = -1.0\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\n            if allocations[i] >= seg['capacity']:\n                continue  # segment saturated\n            # next allocation position = current number + 1 (i.e., the position of the next token)\n            score = seg['importance'] / (allocations[i] + 1)\n            # Tie-break: if score > best_score, or equal but i < best_index (since best_index is set only when better or first)\n            if best_index is None or score > best_score + 1e-12:  # use tolerance for floats\n                best_index = i\n                best_score = score\n            elif abs(score - best_score) < 1e-12:\n                # equal score, prefer smaller index already handled by not updating if equal? Actually if equal and i < best_index we should update. But we only update if score > best_score or if equal and i < best_index. Let's do:\n                if i < best_index:\n                    best_index = i\n                    best_score = score\n        if best_index is None:\n            # Should not happen if budget <= total_cap\n            raise ValueError(\"No segment can accept more tokens, but budget not exhausted\")\n        allocations[best_index] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    total = sum(allocations)\n    by_name = {seg['name']: allocations[i] for i, seg in enumerate(segments)}\n    return {\n        'allocations': allocations,\n        'by_name': by_name,\n        'total': total\n    }\n```\n\nWe need to ensure float comparison tolerance is fine. Use > comparison with small epsilon.\n\nAlso note: the spec says \"Break equal priorities by original segment order.\" So if two have identical score, we pick the earlier index. In my code, I update best_index only if sco